  "issues": [
    {
      "id": "iss_001",
      "title": "Hello, agents",
      "slug": "hello-agents",
      "body": "Coding agents went from autocomplete to something closer to a teammate in about a year, and most of us are still working out what that means day to day.\n\nThat's what The Context Window is for. Every other Thursday, I'll send you one or two things worth trying and one thing worth knowing about.\n\nThanks for being here from the start.",
      "status": "sent",
      "scheduledFor": null,
      "sentAt": "2026-07-26T08:00:00.000Z",
      "createdAt": "2026-07-24T00:00:00.000Z",
      "stats": {
        "recipients": 30,
        "opens": 17,
        "clicks": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "iss_002",
      "title": "The AI tools we actually use",
      "slug": "the-ai-tools-we-actually-use",
      "body": "These are the tools that are still open on our machines at 5pm, rather than the ones from the launch videos.\n\nWe use an agent in the terminal for real changes and a chat window for thinking out loud. For the boring stuff, like renames, commit messages and test data, a small, fast model does the job.\n\nI'd pick the smallest tool that can handle the task and save the bigger models for the work that needs them.",
      "status": "sent",
      "scheduledFor": null,
      "sentAt": "2026-08-09T08:00:00.000Z",
      "createdAt": "2026-08-07T00:00:00.000Z",
      "stats": {
        "recipients": 31,
        "opens": 18,
        "clicks": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "iss_003",
      "title": "Prompts are code now",
      "slug": "prompts-are-code-now",
      "body": "If you run the same prompt every day, it's basically code, so it's worth treating it that way.\n\nWe moved our prompts out of chat history and into the repo. Now when someone improves one, everyone gets the better version, and when one gets worse, we can see what changed.\n\nI'd start with the prompt you've pasted the most this month.",
      "status": "sent",
      "scheduledFor": null,
      "sentAt": "2026-08-23T08:00:00.000Z",
      "createdAt": "2026-08-21T00:00:00.000Z",
      "stats": {
        "recipients": 32,
        "opens": 19,
        "clicks": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "iss_004",
      "title": "Smaller models, same job",
      "slug": "smaller-models-same-job",
      "body": "Ever reached for the biggest model out of habit? Most everyday tasks don't need it.\n\nWe spent a fortnight doing routine work on a smaller model: writing tests, explaining errors and tidying up docs. It kept up on almost all of it, and it was faster.\n\nIt struggled with the long, fuzzy tasks, and that's where the bigger model is worth paying for.",
      "status": "sent",
      "scheduledFor": null,
      "sentAt": "2026-09-06T08:00:00.000Z",
      "createdAt": "2026-09-04T00:00:00.000Z",
      "stats": {
        "recipients": 33,
        "opens": 15,
        "clicks": 4
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "iss_005",
      "title": "Keep your AGENTS.md short",
      "slug": "keep-your-agents-md-short",
      "body": "Your agent reads everything you put in AGENTS.md, so every line should be worth reading.\n\nA short one works better than a long one. Cover how to run the tests, where things live, and the two or three rules you'd tell someone on their first day.\n\nIf you're correcting the same mistake twice, add a line for it.",
      "status": "sent",
      "scheduledFor": null,
      "sentAt": "2026-09-20T08:00:00.000Z",
      "createdAt": "2026-09-18T00:00:00.000Z",
      "stats": {
        "recipients": 34,
        "opens": 20,
        "clicks": 5
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "iss_006",
      "title": "Reviewing agent pull requests",
      "slug": "reviewing-agent-pull-requests",
      "body": "Everyone reads the first pull request an agent opens very carefully. By the tenth, people barely look at them, and that's when things slip through.\n\nI'd review them the way you'd review a new colleague's work. Keep the changes small, ask for a clear description, and make sure the tests prove it does what it says.\n\nIf you can't review it in ten minutes, ask for it in smaller pieces.",
      "status": "sent",
      "scheduledFor": null,
      "sentAt": "2026-10-04T08:00:00.000Z",
      "createdAt": "2026-10-02T00:00:00.000Z",
      "stats": {
        "recipients": 35,
        "opens": 18,
        "clicks": 4
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "iss_007",
      "title": "Reading list: autumn",
      "slug": "reading-list-autumn",
      "body": "Here are a few longer reads for the darker evenings.\n\nThere's a good explainer on how context windows actually work, and why the middle of a long prompt gets less attention than the start and the end.\n\nWe also read about what changes when agents run in the background rather than in your terminal, plus a strong argument for writing the test before you write the prompt.",
      "status": "sent",
      "scheduledFor": null,
      "sentAt": "2026-10-17T23:30:00.000Z",
      "createdAt": "2026-10-16T00:00:00.000Z",
      "stats": {
        "recipients": 36,
        "opens": 18,
        "clicks": 2
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "iss_008",
      "title": "Reading your token bill",
      "slug": "reading-your-token-bill",
      "body": "Your AI bill has three numbers on it, and most of us only look at the total.\n\nInput tokens are what you send and output tokens are what comes back. Cached tokens are input you've sent before, and they cost a fraction of the price, so a prompt that starts the same way every time can end up cheaper than a shorter one that doesn't.\n\nNext time, we'll look at how to see where yours is going.",
      "status": "scheduled",
      "scheduledFor": "2026-12-03T08:00:00.000Z",
      "sentAt": null,
      "createdAt": "2026-10-28T09:05:00.000Z",
      "stats": null
    },
    {
      "id": "iss_009",
      "title": "Café Society",
      "slug": "cafe-society",
      "body": "We spent an evening in a café with a dozen developers and asked them what they actually use AI for at work.\n\nThe answers were a lot less exciting than the headlines, but more useful. People mostly use it to explain unfamiliar code, write the first draft of a test, or turn a vague ticket into a list of questions for the product team.\n\nNobody said they get it to write whole features. Most said it helps them get unstuck faster.",
      "status": "draft",
      "scheduledFor": null,
      "sentAt": null,
      "createdAt": "2026-11-02T10:12:00.000Z",
      "stats": null
    },
    {
      "id": "iss_010",
      "title": "Five things our agent got wrong",
      "slug": "five-things-our-agent-got-wrong",
      "body": "We kept a list for a month, and here's what made it.\n\n1. It said the tests passed, but it hadn't run them.\n\n2. It invented a config option that doesn't exist.\n\n3. It fixed the symptom in one place and left the cause in three others.\n\n4. It rewrote a function we'd only asked it to rename.\n\n5. It apologised, then did the same thing again.\n\nEvery one of these can be fixed with a better instruction, and we'll share ours next time.",
      "status": "draft",
      "scheduledFor": null,
      "sentAt": null,
      "createdAt": "2026-11-06T15:40:00.000Z",
      "stats": null
    }
  ]
